Imagine correcting an article title. The editor shows the new wording, the article page shows the old wording, and the homepage still links to a third version. The interesting caching question is how those views become consistent.

For a content site, I would write down the publishing contract before choosing an API: when does a saved edit become public, how long can an old version remain visible, and what confirms that publication succeeded?

## Start with the publishing model

This portfolio keeps articles in the repository. A local editor saves content files; committing and deploying those files publishes the site. That makes deployment a clear boundary between an edit and its public version.

For that workflow, static generation is a sensible starting point. Adding a live CMS later would change the requirements: a publish event might need to invalidate cached content without rebuilding everything.

My preference is to make that change when there is a real publishing need. An article archive does not automatically need the same freshness model as a shopping cart or an account dashboard.

## Cache Components make the choice explicit

Next.js 16 introduced Cache Components as an opt-in model combining static, cached, and request-time content. The [release announcement](https://nextjs.org/blog/next-16) and [current guide](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/cache-components) explain how the pieces fit together.

With that model enabled, a public article listing is a candidate for shared caching. A visitor's private reading history has different requirements. Request-specific values need deliberate boundaries; they should not accidentally become part of content shared with other visitors.

Before implementing a cache, I would give each data source an entry in a small decision table:

| Data | Who can see it? | What makes it change? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Published article | Everyone | An editorial publish action |
| Article category list | Everyone | Content taxonomy changes |
| Draft content | Authorised editors | Every saved edit |
| Account preferences | One account | That account's settings change |

That exercise often reveals the necessary boundaries more clearly than a list of caching functions.

## Background refresh and immediate feedback differ

The documented `revalidateTag` pattern with the `max` profile allows stale content to be served while fresh content is fetched in the background. A verified CMS webhook can use that behaviour for public content. The call itself is small:

```typescript
import { revalidateTag } from 'next/cache';

// After verifying the publishing webhook and its payload:
revalidateTag('articles', 'max');
```

The [API documentation](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/revalidateTag) describes the stale-while-revalidate behaviour and where the function can run.

An editor saving a change may need to see that exact change immediately. Next.js documents `updateTag` for read-your-own-writes behaviour in Server Actions. It expires tagged data immediately, making the next read wait for fresh data. That is a different contract from background refresh. [The updateTag reference](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/updateTag) explains the scope.

## Test one edit through every surface

I would test publication with a visible, reversible change: amend a title, publish it, and check the article, listing, homepage preview, and feed. Then unpublish the article and verify that the same surfaces agree.

For repository-backed publishing, those checks happen after deployment. For a live CMS, they test invalidation too. Either way, the success criterion is concrete: readers and editors see the version the publishing workflow promises.
